rules_version = '2';

// Firebase Storage rules for the jobs-data-17ee4 bucket.
//
// This bucket holds the year files (jobs-2022.json … jobs-2026.json) — every
// job, invoice number and payment status — the packet files the JTI dashboard
// uploads (invoices, purchase orders, receipts), and the parts diagram images.
//
// Every one of those is money or customer paperwork, and every app that reads
// them signs in first: the Jobs tracker, both parts viewers and the dashboard
// all use email/password. So requiring a signed-in caller costs nothing and is
// the floor.
//
// THIS FILE PREVIOUSLY READ `allow read, write: if true` ON EVERY PATH, with a
// comment noting it. It was not deployed — the live bucket refuses anonymous
// reads — but it sat one `firebase deploy` away from publishing all of the
// above as world read AND write, from the directory you would naturally run
// that command in. The comment was not a safeguard; this is.
//
// NOTE: these rules are written from what the apps need, not read back from the
// deployed bucket. Deploying them is a deliberate act — check the live rules in
// the Firebase console first, because they may well be stricter than this.
//
// A signed-in caller is only a meaningful bar while self-registration is off.
// Public signup is currently ENABLED on this project, so anyone can create an
// account and satisfy `request.auth != null`. Turn it off under
// Authentication → Settings → User actions in the Firebase console; until then
// these rules are a lock on a door that hands out keys.
service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {
    match /{allPaths=**} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
    }
  }
}
